The 2D Gaussian fitting component, which describes the same-side peak, required adjustments in the higher \((y_{t1},y_{t2})\) bins. In these cut bins there is no significant 2D exponential component due to the small contributions from electrons, HBT, or other possible small range correlations.

The same-side peak in cut bins 23-25 deviated from a single Gaussian shape as seen in Fig. 6.5. This new structure was found to be described well by two 2D Gaussian functions, one wide in \(\eta_{\Delta}\) and the other narrow. The same-side peak was then represented by the sum of these two components.

#### Higher Order Fourier Components

The fitting function defined for this analysis contains only the required components to produce a statistically negligible residual. However, higher order harmonics such as \(v_{n}=\langle\cos(n[\phi-\psi_{n}])\rangle\) (n=3,4,...) have been claimed

Figure 6.5: CI angular correlations in the range \(y_{t1}=y_{t2}=[3.5,4.0]\) (cut bin 25) and centrality fraction 9-18%.

